<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sancho I of León</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 935–<a href="/years/966">966</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Sancho the Fat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sancho I, called the Fat, was King of León from 956 to 958 and again from 960 to 966. Deposed by the nobility, he travelled with his grandmother Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toda-of-pamplona">Toda of Navarre</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, where the Jewish physician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdai-ibn-shaprut">Hasdai ibn Shaprut</a> treated his obesity, and with the help of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a> he regained his throne. He was poisoned in 966, reportedly by a Galician count.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sancho-i-of-leon.jpg" alt="Sancho I of León" width="180" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TumboA_%E2%80%93_Sancho_I_of_Le%C3%B3n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sancho was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-ii-of-leon">Ramiro II of León</a> and his second wife, Queen Urraca Sánchez of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>, and a grandson of Sancho I of Pamplona and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toda-of-pamplona">Toda Aznárez</a>. Ramiro II was succeeded by Sancho's elder brother Ordoño III in 951, and at first the younger Sancho disputed the throne with him. When Ordoño died in 956 Sancho took the vacant throne with the support of part of the nobility, his grandmother, and Fernán González, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, who was Ordoño's brother-in-law.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Within two years Sancho was deposed by nobles led by Fernán González because of his extreme obesity, and Ordoño the Wicked, later numbered Ordoño IV, replaced him from 958 to 960. Sancho had also refused to respect the peace that Ordoño III had agreed with the Cordovans, who sent an army against him and defeated him in 957.</p><p>That defeat added to his discredit. He turned to his grandmother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toda-of-pamplona">Toda of Pamplona</a> for help, and in 958 Toda, Sancho and his wife Teresa Ansúrez travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, where a deal was signed with the caliph Abderramán III to help him recover his throne in exchange for some territory on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Duero</a>.</p><p>During his stay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, according to the historian Dozy, Sancho managed to lose some of his weight under the treatment of the court physician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdai-ibn-shaprut">Hasdai ibn Shaprut</a>, who allowed him only infusions for forty days. A Muslim-Pamplonian army took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a> in the spring of 959 and León in the second half of 960, and Ordoño IV fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>.</p><p>Sancho soon forgot his agreement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, who then supported Ordoño IV, though their confrontation went no further than a few punitive raids. The last years of the reign were marked by the growing independence of the Castilian and Galician nobility. In 966 Sancho founded the monastery of San Pelayo in León.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sancho was poisoned and died in 966, not yet 35 years old. According to the chronicle of Sampiro, the rebel count Gonzalo Menéndez gave him a poisoned apple at the Galician monastery of Castrelo de Miño, though not all historians accept that identification, since another count named Gonzalo Muñoz lived at the time. His death fell between 15 November, the date of the last diploma in which he appears, and 19 December, the date of the first diploma of his son Ramiro III, who succeeded him. He was buried at Castrelo de Miño and later moved to León, to the church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> de Palat del Rey.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sancho I of León” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 958 the deposed King <b>Sancho I of León</b>, known as ‘the Fat’, travelled to Córdoba with his grandmother Queen Toda of Navarre to be treated for obesity by Hasdai ibn Shaprut.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 958 the elderly Queen Toda of Pamplona travelled to Córdoba with her grandson <b>Sancho the Fat of León</b>, who was treated there by the caliph’s physician for his obesity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
